  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nConvection occurring from southwest Indiana into eastern Kentucky may\nbrush by some of the Kentucky counties early this morning, but any \nheavier rainfall will most likely not affect those areas. After that,\nit looks like a good part of the day will be mainly dry. There will\nbe a surface boundary from west central Indiana into northeast\nKentucky which will define an instability gradient. Storms may \ndevelop mid to late afternoon with any weak mid level perturbations \ntracking across the area. There could be a few strong to severe \ncells. Heavy rainfall could also occur but looks to be progressive.\n\nA more substantial disturbance in the form of an MCV that originated\nin the Plains will move across the area this evening. Following that,\nthe nose of a modest low level jet will overrun the quasi-stationary\nboundary. Showers and storms are expected to become more numerous \nand elevated north of the boundary. Environment will once again be \nvery favorable for heavy rain with training possible. Various runs of\nguidance are showing rather concerning northwest-southeast oriented \nstreaks of heavy rain through the area, potentially affecting areas \nalready hard hit by flooding. The greatest threat will occur tonight \ninto Friday morning.\n\nIn the wake of this activity, there is not much of a signal for\nadditional storms the rest of Friday, at least as it appears now.\nFlood watch was extended last evening to account for the renewed\nheavy rain potential tonight into Friday morning.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nMid level ridge will build a bit northward over the weekend. This\nwill push the active pattern north of the area. It will also allow\nfor temperatures to warm substantially. There will be the potential\nfor heat indices reaching advisory criteria. Convection will be much\nmore limited until a cold front slides southeast across the region on\nSunday night.",
